ses the  biggest man-eating animal in the world  escapes and our protagonists are the  first prey to cross her path what  follows is a roughly 6 minute action  suspense sequence in which our  protagonists try their best not to get  II seem pretty straightforward right  well yes and no even though the  characters trying to survive is what is  literally happening on the screen it's  what's going on under the surface that  matters from a story perspective it  isn't as much about the introduction  the t-rex as it is about the protagonist  dr. Alan Grant learning to become a  father at the beginning of the film were  introduced to the idea that Dr Grant is  not exactly the kind of guy who lets his  nephew win at Super Smash Brothers in  fact Spielberg really tries to hammer  home that Grant doesn't like kids and  would rather remain obsessed with the  dead past than thinking about creating  life in the future kids have one of  those  we're also briefly notified that  Hammonds daughter of the mom of these  two kids is going through a divorce you  told me that hammy can't even bother to  see me he had to leave early he wants to  be with these daughters she's getting at  the door well I understand that so even  though we're all just anxiously waiting  to see Newman get skeeted on by a  Dilophosaurus the stage is set for the  underlying theme of the movie family  let's head back to that pivotal scene  Donald the only adult in the car with  the kids suddenly feels his breakfast  burrito twirling around in his bowels  and decides to dip out and head to the  nearest bathroom the kids having been  abandoned by yet another male authority  figure are alone and panicking dr. grant  immediately realizes he has to make a  decision he can either remain still and  decrease his chances of getting eaten or  act in a selfless somewhat fatherly  manner Alan one repression a father  instincts zero it's moments like this  that elevate the action from a mere will  they survive - how will the 