 on them  serves a purpose instead of just being a  cop-out cliche to trick the audience  into thinking that the characters have  died  this moment is used to show the  metaphorical rebirth of Allen like a  dinosaur from an egg  he's no longer the dinosaur obsessed  child averse man he once was but instead  has embraced the possibility of being a  future father now that's an action scene  packed with meaning and yeah you've  probably guessed it fallen Kingdom has  nothing like this like Alan and Timmy  Claire and Franklin also barely escaped  their deaths in the nick of time but  that's all there is to it nothing about  the characters or their relationship has  changed and so there's no meaning to it  beyond survival so what could have been  a visualization of their inner growth  ends up being another vapid spectacle  since the characters remain virtually  the same so the reason why this action  scene still grabs us 25 years after its  inception  is not just because it first introduced  us to the t-rex but because of how it  uses action in service of the underlying  story it tells two stories at the same  time one that excites us and adds  suspense and one that people can truly  relate to combine the two and you have  an action sequence that engages audience  across time no matter how prehistoric  its visuals all the pieces were there to  make a thoroughly enjoyable and  memorable film perhaps equaling or even  surpassing the original a film with the  same sort of engaging emotional subtext  as the original combined with the  advancement in digital effects could  have made for something truly special  and yes I'm sure some people had a blast  watching Chris Pratt run away from  dinosaurs and giant melting rocks but  will these scenes still truly live on in  the minds of those same people 25 years  from now needless to say we have doubts  at least Trevor oh and Bayona made sure  that the series wouldn't devolve further  into a mindless monster movie by  repeating the same mistake Spielberg  made in the lost world  Oh now if you're sad that all the real  dinosaurs went the way the fidgets  spinner  don't be there's actually plenty of  prehistoric monsters that still roam the  earth and to the best of my knowledge  none of them will go all noumenon you  take for example the great salamander  who for the past 160 million years has  remained relatively unchanged and  there's a version of it that's so nasty  that early American settlers named it  after hell spawn but to learn all about  that you got to check out the newest  video from Animal Logic if you love  learning about the amazing animals we  share the planet with and I really think  you're gonna like animal logic Danyelle  combines her explanatory powers with her  scientific illustrations to teach you  everything you've ever wanted to know  about animals from the antelope that  looked like they're straight out of Star  Wars to wasps that I honestly never want  to see in my life they're disgusting  if you love animals as much as I do  click here to check out animal logic  thanks for watching guys peace
 

